      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Thieme Medical Publishers 333 Seventh Avenue, New York, NY 10001, USA.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "The purpose of this open, cluster randomized controlled trial was to evaluate whether use of a fiber optic-regulated warming mattress would decrease the incidence of hypothermia in women undergoing cesarean delivery."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "PATIENTS AND METHODS",
            "content": "A total of 484 women were randomized via the cluster method on a rotating weekly basis allocating participants to either use of the warming mattress or the standard method of warming at Parkland Hospital (heat-retaining caps, warmed intravenous and irrigation fluids, and warmed blankets). The primary outcome of interest was maternal hypothermia. Surgical site infections and neonatal outcomes were also assessed."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "The incidence of maternal hypothermia at the conclusion of the surgery was decreased in the warming mattress group, 67 versus 80% in the standard method group (p\u2009=\u20090.013). There were no significant differences in maternal hypothermia at delivery or on arrival to the postanesthesia care unit. The difference in surgical site infections and neonatal outcomes were nonsignificant."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Use of a warming mattress reduced the incidence of maternal hypothermia at the conclusion of surgery; however, on admission to the postanesthesia care unit, these effects had dissipated."
          }
        ]
      },
